%9Setting:%9

    term_hyperlinks (choice: auto, on, off; default: auto)

%9Description:%9

    Makes the web addresses in the text view clickable in terminals that
    know OSC 8 hyperlinks. Addresses starting with http://, https://,
    ftp://, gopher://, gemini://, irc:// and ircs:// become links;
    file:, javascript:, data: and mailto: never do.

    The link is exactly the text that is shown: there are no hidden
    targets. Punctuation after an address (. , ; : ! ? and a closing
    bracket or quote that is not part of it) is left out. Characters that
    may not appear in a URI (non-ASCII letters, \ ^ ` { | }) are
    percent-encoded in the target; spaces and control characters end an
    address, and an address longer than 2048 bytes stays plain text. A
    colour change ends an address too (the rest could be drawn in the
    colour of the background); bold, underline and italic do not. An
    address that wraps over several lines is still one link.

    AUTO makes links only where that is known to be safe:

        - kitty, WezTerm, iTerm2, Ghostty, foot, Alacritty (0.11 and
          later; older versions ignore them), Contour, Windows Terminal,
          Konsole (where its profile allows links), the VS Code terminal
          and VTE 0.50 or later (GNOME Terminal, Tilix, Terminator, ...),
        - inside tmux. tmux 3.4 and later hand the links to the outer
          terminal when it has the "hyperlinks" feature; older versions
          drop them.

    AUTO makes no links under GNU Screen (STY, or a TERM of screen*
    outside tmux), on the Linux, BSD or serial console, for TERM dumb,
    linux, vt*, cons* and Emacs, and for every terminal it cannot
    recognise, such as xterm-256color over ssh: ssh passes on only TERM,
    and a terminal that does not understand OSC could print the
    sequence. ON makes links whatever the terminal is, OFF never.

    How a link is opened depends on the terminal. erssi uses the mouse
    itself, so most terminals want a modifier held while clicking (Ctrl
    or Ctrl+Shift, Cmd on macOS); some show the address when the pointer
    is over it. The side panels, the statusbar and the input line never
    contain links.

%9Examples:%9

    /SET term_hyperlinks on
    /SET term_hyperlinks off

    For tmux 3.4 or later, in ~/.tmux.conf, name the TERM of each outer
    terminal that shows links (tmux matches the TERM of the terminal a
    client is attached from):

        set -as terminal-features ',xterm-kitty:hyperlinks'
        set -as terminal-features ',foot*:hyperlinks'

    Do not use '*': tmux would then send the links to every client,
    also to one attached from a console or a terminal that prints them.
    erssi cannot see the outer terminal from inside tmux, so the choice of
    those terminals is tmux's.

%9See also:%9

    SET, TERM_TRUECOLOR
